<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Negros</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – 1901 CE · capital Bacolod</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Republic of sugar planters on Negros island that broke from Spain in 1898 and soon sought American protection.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 5 November 1898 revolutionaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negros">Negros</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniceto-lacson">Aniceto Lacson</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-araneta">Juan Araneta</a>, rose against the Spanish garrison and secured the island within days. They established a cantonal republic centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacolod">Bacolod</a>, first linked to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-state-of-the-visayas">Federal State of the Visayas</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its leaders were wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> planters who wanted order and access to markets more than full independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniceto-lacson">Lacson</a> served as president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-araneta">Araneta</a> as war secretary. Unlike most revolutionary governments, it welcomed American forces in 1899 and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> supervision, adopting its own constitution.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-military-government-of-the-philippine-islands">United States Military Government of the Philippine Islands</a> gradually absorbed its functions, and the republic was dissolved in April 1901 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negros">Negros</a> became a province under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands">Insular Government of the Philippine Islands</a>. The rising is still commemorated on 5 November.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-negros.jpg" alt="Republic of Negros" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original reconstruction made by RepublicaNegrense. Vector made by Hariboneagle927 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Negros_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bacolod</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aniceto Lacson, Juan Araneta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hiligaynon, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Philippines</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cantonal Republic of Negros</dd></dl></aside></div>
